      "text": "Let's break down the problem and reason through it step-by-step.\n\nGiven that when \\( n \\) is divided by 6, the remainder is 1, we can express \\( n \\) in the form:\n\\[ n = 6k + 1 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nWe are asked to find the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6. Let's substitute \\( n \\) into the expression \\( n + 2010 \\):\n\\[ n + 2010 = (6k + 1) + 2010 \\]\n\\[ n + 2010 = 6k + 2011 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when \\( 6k + 2011 \\) is divided by 6. Notice that \\( 6k \\) is a multiple of 6, so it will be completely divisible by 6. The remainder will depend on the remainder of 2011 when divided by 6.\n\nLet's calculate the remainder of 2011 when divided by 6:\n\\[ 2011 \\div 6 = 335 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\nSo, the remainder when \\( 2011 \\) is divided by 6 is 1.\n\nTherefore, the expression \\( 6k + 2011 \\) can be simplified as:\n\\[ 6k + 2011 \\equiv k + 1 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\nSince \\( k \\) is an integer, the remainder \\( k + 1 \\) will be the same as the remainder \\( n + 2010 \\) when divided by 6.\n\nSo, the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6 is the same as the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 6, which is 1.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]\n\nTo ensure the solution is accurate, let's implement this in Python.\n```python\n# Function to find the remainder when n + 2010 is divided by 6\ndef remainder_when_divided_by_6(k):\n    return (6 * k + 2011) % 6\n\n# Since we know the remainder of n when divided by 6 is 1,\n# we just need to find the remainder of 6k + 2011 divided by 6\nremainder = remainder_when_divided_by_6(1)  # Using k = 1 as we know n = 6k + 1 for some integer k\nprint(remainder)\n```\n\nThe square of an integer is 182 greater than the integer itself. What is the sum of all such integers?\nTo solve the problem, we need to find all the integers \\( x \\) such that \\( x^2 = x + 182 \\). Rearranging this equation, we get:\n\n\\[ x^2 - x - 182 = 0 \\]\