      "text": "To solve this problem, we start by noting that since \\( n \\) leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 6, we can express \\( n \\) as:\n\\[ n = 6k + 1 \\]\nwhere \\( k \\) is an integer. Our goal is to find the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6.\n\nFirst, we substitute the expression for \\( n \\) into \\( n + 2010 \\):\n\\[ n + 2010 = (6k + 1) + 2010 \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ 6k + 2011 \\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the remainder of \\( 6k + 2011 \\) when divided by 6. Since \\( 6k \\) is a multiple of 6, \\( 6k \\) will not contribute to the remainder. Thus, the remainder is solely determined by 2011:\n\\[ 2011 \\div 6 \\]\n\nWe calculate the quotient and the remainder of the division:\n1. Compute the quotient:\n\\[ 2011 \\div 6 = 335 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\nTo verify, we multiply the quotient back by 6:\n\\[ 335 \\times 6 = 2010 \\]\nSubtract this product from 2011:\n\\[ 2011 - 2010 = 1 \\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]",
